OSCAR WINNERS?  BAH!  REAL ACTORS ARE POLITICIANS
By Robert Kirby, The Salt Lake Tribune, March 23, 1999

The moment by which humanity judges its worth has come and gone for
another year.  The Academy Awards ended Sunday night, leaving us all a
bit... annoyed?

Despite everything going on elsewhere in the world, the news media
focused on a group of people, who, although highly creative, behave like
cretins most of the time.  I mean, why do we care what a bunch of people
who can't stay sober or married think and do?

Nowhere is this more odd than when it comes to the emphasis on fashion
at the Academy Awards.  Or, more to the point, female fashion.  The men
could show up in Sears coveralls and no one would notice.

No, the fashion focus is definitely on the actresses.  Mainly on how to
best arrange and present a leading woman's parts without her actually
being arrested.  Ironic when you consider that most Americans, whether
they wanted to or not, have already seen her naked anyway.

Maybe I'm sulking.  My favorite movie of the year was typically ripped
off.  Most of the honors went to "Shakespeare In Love" rather than
"Saving Private Ryan."

This could be a guy thing.  After all, I actually saw "Saving Private
Ryan."  It's essentially a guy flick because it focuses on two important
male behaviors:  shooting and blowing stuff up.

Conversely, I haven't seen "Shakespeare in Love."  Probably because, as
the title suggests, it's about two things guys like me find
incomprehensible:  Shakespeare and love.

The good news is that actor James Coburn won an Oscar.  After years of
being ignored, Coburn finally scored for his supporting role in
"Affliction."

It's about time.  Sheesh, did anyone in the Academy actually watch "The
Magnificent Seven" back in 1960?

Personally, I suspect the Academy Awards is just a huge plot designed to
confuse the public about the true nature of acting.  For real drama,
serious acting, you have to turn to politics.

Regardless of what you saw Sunday night, the political arena is where
America's best actors are found.  For true role-playing, you simply
cannot beat politicians.  More's the pity, frankly.

Yes, Hollywood types are great.  But despite their best efforts, they
all fall short when it comes to really overcoming their inhibitions and
getting into their roles.  Has anyone ever seen John Wayne cry in a
movie?  Has anyone ever seen Meryl Streep not cry in one?  Now that
would be real acting.

The shortfall has to be the creative flow.  It's much stronger on
Capitol Hill.  No surprise.  When it comes to creative motivation, what
is winning an Oscar compared to the possibility of going to prison?

A perfect example of this was Bill Clinton's stellar performance in
denying his affair with Monica.  Oh, sure, nobody believed him, but that
is not the point.  How many people believe Travis actually shot Old
Yeller?

No, by relying on his improvisational skills, Bill was able to wrangle
an epic out of a quickie.  Best of all, America was absolutely riveted
by the performance.  We followed it faithfully, even neglecting our
children to keep up on the changing hairdos of Hillary and Monica.

So powerful was this rendition that Hollywood is already working on a
screen version.  That says a lot about who is the best, doesn't it?  I
mean, when was the last time you saw an American president play the role
of Paulie Shore or Sylvester Stallone?

We should pay more attention to the roles our politicians play rather
than the roles you see rewarded at the Academy Awards.  The worst that
can happen there is we will be bored to the point of gnawing on the
furniture.  Conversely, when was the last time an actor or actress
raised taxes, changed laws, or started a war?

OK, besides Ronald Reagan.

