THE MOST UNRESTRAINED OSCAR PERFORMANCES HAPPEN IN OUR FAMILY ROOMS
by Mickey Guisewite (View from the Middle) (March 14, 1999)

"What are those sparkly things in her hair?"

"Who cares about her hair?  Where's the top half of her dress?"

"I heard she paid $30,000 for that gown."

"That's about $15,000 per square inch of fabric."

Good evening, and welcome to the Oscars, with your hosts, the millions
of women across America who have plopped ourselves down in front of the
television and are ready to cut loose with our comments.

"Nose job."

"Boob job."

"Face-lift. "

"Toupee."

Join us for scintillating commentary on the film industry:  "Is that her
rear end or is her dress sticking out?"

Stay with us for thought-provoking questions about the actors:  "Is
what's-his-name still dating what's-her-name?"

But mostly, tune in for the sort of minute-by-minute Oscar analysis that
can only happen when a woman has taken off her pantyhose and is sitting
on her family room couch in a sweat suit with a bag of Cheetos and a
diet soda balanced on her knee:

"Her hair looks like she dumped a bottle of olive oil on it."

"Eleven months to prepare for the Oscars and that's what she wears?"

I have a theory regarding Oscar viewers.  I believe that as an audience,
we respond to the nominees not based on how they look, but on how we
look.  It's a sliding scale of personal appearance and commentary in
which the more slovenly we are, the more opinionated we become.

WOMAN AT AN OSCAR PARTY WEARING A COCKTAIL DRESS:  "Kim Basinger looks
smashing."

WOMAN AT A CASUAL OSCAR GET-TOGETHER WEARING JEANS:  "I think Kim
Basinger looked better last year."

WOMAN AT HOME ALONE ON HER COUCH WEARING A ROBE, FUZZY SLIPPERS, AND
GLASSES HELD TOGETHER WITH MASKING TAPE:  "Get a life, Kim Basinger!  I
wouldn't be caught dead in that outfit!"

Similarly, our Oscar comments correspond directly to the amount of
calories we happen to be consuming at the time.

WOMAN NIBBLING ON A SESAME TRIANGLE AND SIPPING A PELLEGRINO:  "Harrison
Ford is the most appealing man on the planet."

WOMAN SPLITTING A BOWL OF POPCORN WITH A GIRLFRIEND:  "Do you think
Harrison Ford's attractive?"

WOMAN ALONE IN HER LIVING ROOM USING AN EMPTY PIZZA BOX AS A TRAY FOR
HER ICE CREAM:  "In your wildest dreams, Harrison!  Like I'd ever go out
with you!"

I've been an official Oscar viewer for 20 years.  I've watched them
while dressed up and dressed down.  My favorite way to watch the Oscars,
though, is the way I plan to watch them this year -- in the family room
wearing a pair of sweat pants, sweat socks and the top of my husband's
flannel pajamas.

There I will sprawl, hair pulled back, makeup off, glasses on, gesturing
with fistfuls of popcorn while proclaiming my opinions of the steady
stream of perfectly coiffed actors and actresses parading onto the
stage.

Honestly.  People make such spectacles of themselves, don't they?

